//! C ABI for native frontends.
//!
//! The only crate frontends link against. Exposes opaque handles and plain C
//! functions; never leaks Rust types, never unwinds across the boundary. The
//! header `TesseraCore.h` is generated by `cbindgen` at build time and bundled
//! into `TesseraCore.xcframework` for the macOS app.
//!
//! # Shape of the API
//!
//! * One opaque [`TesseraApp`](App) per process, from `tessera_app_new`,
//!   released with `tessera_app_free`.
//! * Sessions are `uint64_t` ids handed out by `tessera_session_spawn`, never
//!   pointers; an id that is stale or foreign yields
//!   `TESSERA_ERROR_SESSION_NOT_FOUND` rather than undefined behaviour.
//! * Every fallible function returns a [`TesseraError`](Error) and reports
//!   results through out-parameters.
//! * The core reports events through a [`TesseraCallbacks`](Callbacks)
//!   table; the frontend never polls.
//! * Keyboard, mouse, scroll, paste and focus events go in through the
//!   `tessera_session_send_*`, `tessera_session_paste` and
//!   `tessera_session_set_focus` functions as plain structs (see the
//!   `input` module); the core decides between key bindings and PTY input.
//! * Text selection, Copy and command-click links go through the
//!   `tessera_session_select_*`, `tessera_session_selection_text`,
//!   `tessera_session_link_at` and `tessera_session_set_hover` functions
//!   (see the `selection` module), in view-relative cells.
//! * Scrollback search goes through the `tessera_session_search_*`
//!   functions (see the `search` module): set a query, step it from the
//!   display link, move between matches, copy them all.
//! * A session is drawn into a host-owned `CAMetalLayer` through the
//!   `tessera_surface_*` functions (see the `surface` module): attach,
//!   resize, render each display refresh, set the font size, detach. The
//!   session's grid follows the surface's size.
//! * Every export runs inside `catch_unwind` (see the `panic` module): a
//!   panic becomes `TESSERA_ERROR_PANIC`, or is logged and swallowed by
//!   functions that return nothing.
//!
//! The thread-safety contract is documented on [`App`] and [`Callbacks`],
//! and is reproduced in the generated header.

